import pf.Html
import ../../../Demos/PushCompose
import ../../../Demos/PushStates
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Demo
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Note
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths

## The push package, a plugin: how Web Push goes, the package's example app file by file, `<PushButton>` and
## its states, a notification and its options (live: the package's functions in the browser),
## sending, the modules, and what works today and what waits on a proposal. Static but for the
## notification demo: this site is files, and a push needs a server.
meta = {
	title: "Push",
	description: "Web Push notifications for Roc Solid apps, as a plugin: a button that subscribes, a table that keeps each browser's subscription, and jobs that send. Sending waits on the server's host.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "how", label: "How it works" },
		{ id: "example", label: "A first app" },
		{ id: "button", label: "<PushButton>" },
		{ id: "notification", label: "A notification" },
		{ id: "sending", label: "Sending" },
		{ id: "modules", label: "The modules" },
		{ id: "safety", label: "Safety" },
		{ id: "status", label: "Status" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/push</code>]}
				title="Notifications that arrive with every tab closed"
			>
				Web Push lets a server send a notification to a browser that said yes, even when none of the
				site's tabs is open. This package has each part, a plugin an app lists in one line: a button that
				asks and subscribes, a table that keeps each browser's subscription, and a job that sends to it. One
				piece is missing: the server's host can't yet do the encryption Web Push needs, so today every send fails and says why (see
				<a href="#status">Status</a>).
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="how">How it works</Heading>
			<ol>
				<li>
					A click on <code>{"<PushButton>"}</code> asks the browser's permission. Browsers ask only during a
					click, a key press or a tap.
				</li>
				<li>
					The browser subscribes at its push service (Google's for Chrome, Mozilla's for Firefox, Apple's
					for Safari, Microsoft's for Edge), with the server's public key. It gets a subscription: a URL at
					that service, and two keys.
				</li>
				<li>The page posts the subscription to the app, which keeps it in a table: a row for each browser.</li>
				<li>Later, the server calls <code>Push.notify!</code>, which queues a job for each of the user's subscriptions.</li>
				<li>
					The job encrypts the notification for that one browser (RFC 8291), signs a token that says which
					server sent it (VAPID, RFC 8292), and posts both to the subscription's URL (RFC 8030).
				</li>
				<li>
					The push service wakes the browser. The package's service worker shows the notification, and a
					click on it opens the page it names.
				</li>
			</ol>
			<p>The package comes in three parts:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Plain Roc</strong>, on no platform: the formats, the key derivation, the token, and what
					each answer means. <code>roc test packages/push/main.roc</code> checks it against the RFCs' own
					examples. The browser's half sees these too (<code>browser.roc</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The plugin</strong>, <code>{"Push.plugin({})"}</code>: its endpoints, a table that keeps
					subscriptions in the app's database (Postgres or SQLite), and a job that sends, which the
					<a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>jobs</a> plugin runs (packages/jobs, which the app lists too). It
					uses the server platform, which the package names in its header
					(<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugins</a>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The browser's part</strong>: <code>{"<PushButton>"}</code> (in <code>components/</code>,
					which <code>rocx build</code> copies into the app), the page's script and the service worker (in
					<code>static/</code>), which the plugin serves: the worker at the site's root, so its scope is the
					whole site, and the script on every page.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="example">A first app</Heading>
			<p>
				The package has a small app, <code>packages/push/example</code>: a page with the button and a button
				that notifies everyone. Each file below is that app's. This site can't run it: the site is static
				files, and Web Push needs a server.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>1. Keys.</strong> <code>node packages/push/vapid-keys.mjs</code> prints a key pair. Give the
				server <code>VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY</code>, <code>VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY</code> and <code>VAPID_SUBJECT</code>
				(<code>mailto:</code> and an address, or an <code>https:</code> URL) in its environment. The private
				key never leaves the server. A browser that subscribed with one public key can only be sent to with
				that pair. The plugin needs all three: without one, or with a pair that isn't one, the server doesn't
				start, and says why.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>2. The plugin</strong>, in the app's header and its plugins, with the jobs plugin, which runs
				its job:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.push_example_main.file} source={Sources.push_example_main.rocx} />
			<p>
				That is all the server needs. The plugin answers <code>GET /push/key</code>, <code>POST
				/push/subscribe</code> and <code>POST /push/unsubscribe</code> behind the app's middleware; makes its
				table; runs <code>push.deliver</code> on the jobs plugin's workers; and serves the service worker at
				<code>/push-sw.js</code> and the page's script, which every page loads, before the app's middleware,
				so a guard never blocks them. <code>{"Push.plugin({ at: \"/notifications\" })"}</code> puts the
				endpoints elsewhere; <code>{"Push.plugin({ owner: |context| context.member })"}</code> makes each
				subscription the signed-in user's, as the app's middleware hands it on, where without it each is a
				visitor's (<code>""</code>).
			</p>
			<p><strong>3. The page</strong>: the button, and a handler that asks an endpoint to notify everyone.</p>
			<Code title={Sources.push_example_page.file} source={Sources.push_example_page.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.push_example_announce.file} source={Sources.push_example_announce.rocx} />

			<Heading id="button">{"<PushButton>"}</Heading>
			<p>
				A button that turns this browser's notifications on, and off again. A click calls the plugin's script
				(<code>static/push.js</code>) with <a href={"${Paths.components}#effects"}><code>Browser.call!</code></a>.
				The script asks permission, registers the service worker, subscribes with the server's key, and
				posts the subscription. Turning notifications off unsubscribes, and tells the server.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Prop</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>api : Str</code></td>
							<td>
								where the app's push endpoints are: with <code>"/push"</code>, <code>GET /push/key</code> (the
								public key), <code>POST /push/subscribe</code> and <code>POST /push/unsubscribe</code>
							</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				The script says how a click went in a <code>pushstate</code> event, which every
				<code>{"<PushButton>"}</code> on the page hears. When the page wakes up, the script also says where
				this browser stands. The button reads each state with the package's <code>PushState</code>:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>The script says</th><th>The button shows</th></tr></thead>
					<PushStates />
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>aria-pressed</code> on the button says whether notifications are on, and a screen reader reads
				the note when it changes (<code>aria-live</code>). On an iPhone or an iPad, a site has Web Push only
				once it is added to the Home Screen and opened from there.
			</p>
			<details>
				<summary>The button: <code>{Sources.push_button.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.push_button.file} source={Sources.push_button.rocx} />
			</details>

			<Heading id="notification">A notification</Heading>
			<p>
				A notification is a title, a body, the page a click opens (<code>url</code>) and a <code>tag</code>:
				a newer notification with the same tag replaces an older one. Its options tell the push service how to
				treat it. Change them below: the package's own functions, running in your browser, show what a server
				would send.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo>
			<div data-demo="push-compose"><PushCompose /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<Note>
				The request has two more headers: <code>Authorization</code> (the VAPID token, <code>vapid t=…, k=…</code>)
				and <code>Content-Encoding: aes128gcm</code>. The data travels encrypted: the push service sees
				only bytes.
			</Note>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>title : Str</code></td><td>the notification's first line</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>body : Str</code></td><td>the text under it</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>url : Str</code></td><td>the page a click opens, on this site only; empty: the front page</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>tag : Str</code></td><td>a newer notification with the same tag replaces this one, rather than piling up</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>icon : Str</code></td><td>an image's URL</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>{"Notification.new(title, body)"}</code> makes one with the rest empty, and
				<code>{"{ ..Notification.new(title, body), url: \"/orders/17\" }"}</code> sets more. Empty fields are
				left out of the JSON.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>ttl : U64</code></td>
							<td>
								seconds the push service keeps the message for a browser that is offline: 0 is now or never,
								and more than four weeks is sent as four weeks
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>urgency : [VeryLow, Low, Normal, High]</code></td>
							<td>how soon it must arrive: a phone on battery may hold the low ones back</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>topic : Str</code></td>
							<td>
								a newer message with the same topic replaces one still waiting, so only the latest status
								arrives: 32 characters at most, of letters, digits, <code>-</code> and <code>_</code>
							</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p><code>Options.defaults</code> is a day, <code>Normal</code>, and no topic.</p>
			<details>
				<summary>This demo: <code>{Sources.push_compose.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.push_compose.file} source={Sources.push_compose.rocx} />
			</details>

			<Heading id="sending">Sending</Heading>
			<p>
				On the server, a job or an endpoint calls one of these. Each checks the
				notification and its options, then queues a <code>push.deliver</code> job for each subscription, in
				the caller's transaction when there is one, and answers how many.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.push_send.file} source={Sources.push_send.rocx} />
			<p>A job worker sends each message. The push service's answer says what happens next:</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>The push service answers</th><th>The job</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>201, or any 2xx</td><td>is done, and notes when it sent</td></tr>
						<tr><td>404 or 410</td><td>deletes the subscription: the browser unsubscribed, or it expired</td></tr>
						<tr><td>429 or 5xx, with <code>Retry-After</code></td><td>sends it again then, five times at most, if the message's TTL lasts</td></tr>
						<tr><td>408, 429 or 5xx, or no answer</td><td>fails the attempt, and the worker tries again, waiting longer each time (five attempts)</td></tr>
						<tr><td>400, 401, 403, 413, or anything else</td><td>logs it and notes it on the subscription: sending again wouldn't help</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A retry sends the TTL that is left, so a message never outlives its TTL. A 403 doesn't delete the
				subscription: push services answer 403 when the VAPID keys are wrong, and a mistake in the server's
				settings shouldn't delete every subscription.
			</p>

			<Heading id="modules">The modules</Heading>
			<p>
				<strong>Plain Roc</strong>, on no platform, which both halves of an app see.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Subscription</code></td><td>a browser's subscription: <code>parse</code> (the page's JSON), <code>from_body</code> (an endpoint's body), <code>from_text</code> and <code>to_text</code> (a table row), <code>to_json</code>; <code>known_service</code>: is it at Chrome's, Firefox's, Safari's or Edge's push service?</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Notification</code></td><td>what the service worker shows: <code>new</code>, <code>to_json</code>, <code>data</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Options</code></td><td>how a push service treats a message: <code>defaults</code>, <code>check</code> (the topic), <code>headers</code>, <code>remaining</code> (the TTL left for a retry)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Delivery</code></td><td><code>request</code> (the POST to a subscription's URL); <code>outcome</code> (what an answer means: <code>Delivered</code>, <code>Gone</code>, <code>RetryAfter(ms)</code>, <code>Retry(why)</code>, <code>Refused(why)</code>), <code>retry_after</code>, <code>http_date</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Vapid</code></td><td>the server's token, RFC 8292: <code>Keys</code> and <code>from_text</code> (from the environment), <code>audience</code>, <code>claims</code> (12 hours), <code>signing_input</code>, <code>token</code>, <code>authorization</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>WebPush</code></td><td>the message's encryption, RFC 8291: <code>prepare</code> (everything before the AES), <code>body</code>, <code>max_data</code> (3993 bytes)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Ece</code></td><td>the <code>aes128gcm</code> content coding, RFC 8188: <code>header</code>, <code>parse_header</code>, <code>pad</code>, <code>unpad</code>, <code>keys</code>, <code>nonce</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td>(crypto)</td><td>HMAC-SHA-256, HKDF-SHA-256 and base64url come from <a href={Paths.ecosystem_crypto}>the crypto package</a>, which push names in its header</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>PushState</code></td><td>where a browser stands, as the page's script says it: what <code>{"<PushButton>"}</code> shows</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<strong>The server's part</strong>, on the server platform, with the table's migrations
				(<code>migrations/</code>, Postgres's and SQLite's). Each function's <code>owner</code> is the app's id
				for a user, as text, or <code>""</code> for a visitor.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Push</code></td><td><code>plugin(setup)</code>, the plugin (<code>at</code>, <code>owner</code>); <code>subscribe!(body, owner)</code> and <code>unsubscribe!(endpoint, owner)</code>, for the endpoints; <code>notify!</code> and <code>broadcast!</code>; <code>public_key!</code> and <code>keys!</code> (from the environment); <code>deliver</code>, the job; <code>send!</code>, one attempt</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>PushStore</code></td><td>the <code>push_subscriptions</code> table: <code>save!</code> (a row for each endpoint), <code>remove!</code>, <code>find!</code>, <code>of_owner!</code>, <code>everyone!</code>, and what the job notes</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p><strong>The browser's files</strong>, <code>static/</code>, which the plugin serves:</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>File</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>push.js</code></td><td>the page's script, at <code>/rocx/plugins/push/push.js</code>, on every page: the <code>push.subscribe</code> and <code>push.unsubscribe</code> effects the button calls; once a visit, it sends the server the browser's subscription again. Under a base (<code>/rocx/</code>), every URL is under it.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>push-sw.js</code></td><td>the service worker, at the site's root (<code>/push-sw.js</code>): shows a message's notification; a click focuses a tab at its URL, or opens one; when the push service replaces a subscription, it tells the server</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Heading id="safety">Safety</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					The server sends a request to whatever URL a subscription names. So <code>Push.subscribe!</code>
					takes only <code>https:</code> URLs at the browsers' push services. Under <code>--dev</code>, it also
					takes one on this machine, for a stand-in push service.
				</li>
				<li>The private key never leaves the server. The page gets only the public key.</li>
				<li>A click on a notification opens only this site's pages.</li>
				<li>Messages are encrypted for the browser: the push service sees bytes.</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="status">Status</Heading>
			<p>What works today:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					Everything that is plain Roc, checked by <code>expect</code>s against the RFCs' own examples: the
					keys, the VAPID token's claims and signing input, the content coding, the message's key derivation
					(up to the AES), the request's headers, and what each answer means.
				</li>
				<li>
					The plugin, built into an app and run against SQLite, and Postgres when there is one, and a stand-in push service: it saves and
					forgets subscriptions, queues a job for each, and handles every answer.
				</li>
				<li>
					The button, the page's script and the service worker, tried in Chromium behind a guard that refuses
					every script the app would answer: the plugin's come all the same, the worker's scope is the whole
					site, and the button subscribes.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>What waits on a proposal, in <code>packages/push/PROPOSALS.md</code>:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Sending.</strong> The server's host has no P-256 (the key agreement that encrypts a
					message, and the signature on the VAPID token), and Web Push needs AES-128-GCM with a nonce it
					derives. Until the host has them, every delivery fails and says so. The proposal sketches them on
					<code>ring</code>, which the host already links, and the sketch passes the RFCs' examples.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Jobs that give up, or come back at a time.</strong> Today a refused message ends its job
					as if it were sent, and a <code>Retry-After</code> queues a copy of the job for then.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Browser.call!</code> with an answer.</strong> Until a call can answer, the script
					tells the button how it went with a DOM event (<code>pushstate</code>).
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				And a limit: a message holds 3993 bytes of data, the notification's JSON. <code>Push.notify!</code>
				refuses a longer one.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
